;; scope-alu.expected.sexp — the expected observations for `scope-alu.s` (P2-SCALAR.1).
;; Validate with:
;;   python3 scripts/check_sexp_schema.py scope-alu.expected.sexp schema/expectations.sexp

(comment "scope-alu.expected.sexp — the expected observations for `scope-alu.s`." "" "⛔ EVERY VALUE HERE WAS DERIVED FROM THE PINNED SPECIFICATION PROSE BEFORE THE PROGRAM WAS RUN." "`EVD-05` and `docs/EVIDENCE_AND_GATES.md` require it: an expected value copied out of a model's" "output tests that the model is self-consistent and nothing else. The `derivation` field on each" "step is the reasoning, and `source` is where in the pinned artifacts the rule is stated." "" "The operand builders (steps 0–4) reuse forms the earlier guests already pin; steps 5–30 are the" "scope-completion observations — the 21 remaining ALU forms and FENCE, each against its decision." "⚠️ Two comparisons produce 0, and the laboratory's observation vocabulary is the VISIBLE register" "change: a 0 written into an already-zero register would leave no observation. Each is therefore" "pre-written with 1, so the result appears as a 1→0 transition (steps 12–13 and 15–16).")
(expectations (program "scope-alu.s") (entry "0x80000000") (instructions 31) (step (n 0) (insn "addi x1, x0, -1") (writes (write (reg "x1") (value "0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF"))) (derivation "x0 is hardwired to zero; the 12-bit immediate -1 sign-extends to all ones at 64 bits.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.1 (x0 hardwired); §1.1.4 (I-immediate sign-extension)")) (step (n 1) (insn "addi x2, x0, 0x654") (writes (write (reg "x2") (value "0x0000000000000654"))) (derivation "0x654 is positive and fits the 12-bit immediate, so the sign extension adds no high bits.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.4")) (step (n 2) (insn "addi x3, x0, 0x470") (writes (write (reg "x3") (value "0x0000000000000470"))) (derivation "As step 1: 0x470 is a small positive immediate.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.4")) (step (n 3) (insn "lui x4, 0x80000") (writes (write (reg "x4") (value "0xFFFFFFFF80000000"))) (derivation "The U-immediate 0x80000 occupies bits 31..12, giving the 32-bit value 0x80000000; LUI sign-extends it to 64 bits and bit 31 is set. This is the operand with bit 63 AND bit 31 set that the *W and shift steps below need.") (source "RVI-RV64I §3.1.2.1 (D-LUI-AUIPC)")) (step (n 4) (insn "addi x5, x0, 32") (writes (write (reg "x5") (value "0x0000000000000020"))) (derivation "32 is the shift amount whose low-6-bit reading (32) and low-5-bit reading (0) differ — the register the SLLW step uses to pin D-SHAMT's 5-bit rule for the *W shifts.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.4")) (step (n 5) (insn "and x6, x2, x3") (writes (write (reg "x6") (value "0x0000000000000450"))) (derivation "0x654 = 0b0110_0101_0100 and 0x470 = 0b0100_0111_0000; the bitwise AND keeps only the shared ones: 0b0100_0101_0000 = 0x450.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.4 (AND is bitwise)")) (step (n 6) (insn "or x7, x2, x3") (writes (write (reg "x7") (value "0x0000000000000674"))) (derivation "The bitwise OR keeps every one from either operand: 0b0110_0111_0100 = 0x674.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.4")) (step (n 7) (insn "xor x8, x2, x3") (writes (write (reg "x8") (value "0x0000000000000224"))) (derivation "The bitwise XOR keeps the bits that differ: 0b0010_0010_0100 = 0x224. AND, OR and XOR of one operand pair give three different values, which is why all three are here.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.4")) (step (n 8) (insn "andi x9, x4, -1") (writes (write (reg "x9") (value "0xFFFFFFFF80000000"))) (derivation "The immediate -1 sign-extends to all ones, so ANDI returns rs1 unchanged: 0xFFFFFFFF80000000. A zero-extending reader would compute rs1 & 0xFFF = 0 — the sign extension is the observation.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.4 (D-ALU-IMM: the immediate is sign-extended)")) (step (n 9) (insn "ori x10, x4, 0x7FF") (writes (write (reg "x10") (value "0xFFFFFFFF800007FF"))) (derivation "OR against the sign-extended positive immediate 0x7FF sets the low 11 bits: 0xFFFFFFFF80000000 | 0x7FF = 0xFFFFFFFF800007FF.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.4")) (step (n 10) (insn "xori x11, x4, -1") (writes (write (reg "x11") (value "0x000000007FFFFFFF"))) (derivation "XOR against all ones inverts every bit of rs1: ~0xFFFFFFFF80000000 = 0x000000007FFFFFFF.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.4")) (step (n 11) (insn "slt x12, x1, x2") (writes (write (reg "x12") (value "0x0000000000000001"))) (derivation "Signed comparison: x1 holds -1 and x2 holds 1620, and -1 < 1620, so the result is 1.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.4 (SLT is the signed comparison)")) (step (n 12) (insn "addi x13, x0, 1") (writes (write (reg "x13") (value "0x0000000000000001"))) (derivation "The pre-write: the next step's result is 0, and the laboratory's observation vocabulary is the VISIBLE register change — a 0 written into an already-zero register would be invisible. Writing 1 first makes the comparison's answer observable as a 1→0 transition.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.4")) (step (n 13) (insn "sltu x13, x1, x2") (writes (write (reg "x13") (value "0x0000000000000000"))) (derivation "The same operands as step 11, read unsigned: x1 is 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, the largest 64-bit value, which is not below 1620, so the result is 0 — observed as the 1→0 transition the pre-write arranged. Steps 11 and 13 are the signed/unsigned pair on one register pair.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.4 (SLTU is the unsigned comparison)")) (step (n 14) (insn "slti x14, x4, 1") (writes (write (reg "x14") (value "0x0000000000000001"))) (derivation "x4 = 0xFFFFFFFF80000000 is negative as a signed 64-bit value, so it compares below 1: result 1.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.4")) (step (n 15) (insn "addi x15, x0, 1") (writes (write (reg "x15") (value "0x0000000000000001"))) (derivation "The pre-write for the next step's 0 result, exactly as step 12.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.4")) (step (n 16) (insn "sltiu x15, x4, 1") (writes (write (reg "x15") (value "0x0000000000000000"))) (derivation "The same comparison read unsigned: 0xFFFFFFFF80000000 is enormous, not below 1: result 0, observed as a 1→0 transition.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.4")) (step (n 17) (insn "sltiu x16, x4, -1") (writes (write (reg "x16") (value "0x0000000000000001"))) (derivation "⭐ The SLTIU rule the profile names in D-ALU-IMM: the immediate is SIGN-extended first — -1 becomes 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF — and only then compared unsigned. 0xFFFFFFFF80000000 < 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, so the result is 1. A reader that zero-extended the immediate (0xFFF) would answer 0.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.4 (SLTIU sign-extends the immediate, then compares unsigned — D-ALU-IMM)")) (step (n 18) (insn "sub x17, x2, x3") (writes (write (reg "x17") (value "0x00000000000001E4"))) (derivation "0x654 - 0x470 = 0x1E4, no borrow, no wrap.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.4 (SUB subtracts; overflow is ignored)")) (step (n 19) (insn "sub x18, x3, x2") (writes (write (reg "x18") (value "0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFE1C"))) (derivation "The same pair reversed: 0x470 - 0x654 = -0x1E4, which wraps modulo 2^64 to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFE1C. Overflow is ignored and the result wraps.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.4; RVI-INTRO, Memory (arithmetic wraps modulo 2^XLEN — D-ADDR-WRAP)")) (step (n 20) (insn "sll x19, x3, x2") (writes (write (reg "x19") (value "0x0000000047000000"))) (derivation "D-SHAMT: only the low 6 bits of rs2 are the amount — 0x654 & 0x3F = 0x14 = 20. 0x470 << 20 = 0x47000000.") (source "RVI-RV64I §3.1.2.2 (D-SHAMT: the low 6 bits of rs2 at XLEN=64)")) (step (n 21) (insn "srl x20, x4, x2") (writes (write (reg "x20") (value "0x00000FFFFFFFF800"))) (derivation "The same amount (20), logical: zeros enter from the top, so 0xFFFFFFFF80000000 >> 20 = 0x00000FFFFFFFF800.") (source "RVI-RV64I §3.1.2.2")) (step (n 22) (insn "sra x21, x4, x2") (writes (write (reg "x21") (value "0xFFFFFFFFFFFFF800"))) (derivation "The same operand and amount, arithmetic: the sign bit replicates, so -0x80000000 >> 20 = -0x800 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFF800. The SRL/SRA pair on one negative operand is the observation.") (source "RVI-RV64I §3.1.2.2")) (step (n 23) (insn "addw x22, x4, x2") (writes (write (reg "x22") (value "0xFFFFFFFF80000654"))) (derivation "D-WSUFFIX: the upper 32 bits of both operands are ignored — 0x80000000 + 0x654 = 0x80000654 — and the 32-bit sum is sign-extended; bit 31 is set, so the upper half becomes all ones.") (source "RVI-RV64I §3.1.2.2 (D-WSUFFIX)")) (step (n 24) (insn "subw x23, x4, x2") (writes (write (reg "x23") (value "0x000000007FFFF9AC"))) (derivation "The same low halves subtracted: 0x80000000 - 0x654 = 0x7FFFF9AC, whose bit 31 is CLEAR, so the sign extension leaves a positive 64-bit value. Steps 23 and 24 pin both sign-extension outcomes.") (source "RVI-RV64I §3.1.2.2 (D-WSUFFIX)")) (step (n 25) (insn "sllw x24, x2, x5") (writes (write (reg "x24") (value "0x0000000000000654"))) (derivation "⭐ D-SHAMT's *W rule: the shift amount is rs2[4:0], not rs2[5:0]. x5 holds 32, which a 5-bit read sees as 0, so nothing shifts and the result is the sign extension of the low 32 bits of x2: 0x654. A 6-bit reader would shift left by 32 and answer 0x0000065400000000.") (source "RVI-RV64I §3.1.2.2 (the *W shifts use rs2[4:0])")) (step (n 26) (insn "srlw x25, x4, x2") (writes (write (reg "x25") (value "0x0000000000000800"))) (derivation "The low 32 bits of x4 are 0x80000000; shifted right logically by 20 within 32 bits: 0x800. Bit 31 of the 32-bit result is clear, so the sign extension to 64 bits adds nothing.") (source "RVI-RV64I §3.1.2.2")) (step (n 27) (insn "sraw x26, x4, x2") (writes (write (reg "x26") (value "0xFFFFFFFFFFFFF800"))) (derivation "The same low half and amount, arithmetic within 32 bits: bit 31 (NOT bit 63) replicates, giving the 32-bit result 0xFFFFF800, which then sign-extends to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFF800.") (source "RVI-RV64I §3.1.2.2")) (step (n 28) (insn "srliw x27, x4, 31") (writes (write (reg "x27") (value "0x0000000000000001"))) (derivation "D-SHAMT: the *W immediate shifts take a 5-bit amount. 0x80000000 >>> 31 = 1.") (source "RVI-RV64I §3.1.2.1 (D-SHAMT: a 5-bit amount for the *W shifts)")) (step (n 29) (insn "sraiw x28, x4, 31") (writes (write (reg "x28") (value "0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF"))) (derivation "The same immediate and operand, arithmetic: bit 31 fills the whole 32-bit word (0xFFFFFFFF), and the *W sign extension then fills the upper half too. Contrast with step 28.") (source "RVI-RV64I §3.1.2.1")) (step (n 30) (insn "fence 0, 15, 15, x0, x0") (writes) (derivation "D-FENCE: FENCE is decoded and must not trap, and with one hart, no devices and an in-order model it has no observable effect — so the expectation is the EMPTY writes table: the step happens and nothing changes. fm=0 with pred=succ=iorw is the canonical full fence; the reserved fm/pred/succ configurations execute as FENCE with fm=0000 by the architecture's own mandate (D-FENCE, corrected by P2-SCALAR.3) and are pinned by fault-fence.") (source "RVI-RV32I §1.1.7 (D-FENCE)")))
